00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.509 align:start position:0%
 
good morning everyone  my friend  joginder singh  is in ninja  no he doesn't specialize in covert  warfare like these guys do  but he is fighting a war  he's fighting a war against banks and  traditional financial institutions  who don't find him loanworthy  they call him a ninja  because according to them  he has no income  no job and no assets  i repeat  joginder cannot provide the paperwork to  banks that they require  so they call him a ninja with no income  no job and no assets  but joginder does have a job and he does  have assets he just doesn't have  paperwork to prove for it  he's a farmer in northwestern india in  punjab  in fact he provides jobs for 15  labourers on his farm every year  now joginder is a progressive farmer  he wants to get a loan for his first  tractor to increase the yield to  increase the output of his farm  but banks won't fund him  so at this point i want to ask the  audience show of hands  how many of you have heard of micro  finance  this is stanford so this i'd say that's  about 100 of you nearly  great well it turns out joginder has  approached microfinance companies as  well  and  they don't really meet his need  unfortunately because a tractor costs  nearly ten thousand dollars in india  and uh microfinance companies typically  don't go beyond five six thousand  dollars in funding  so he's stuck  well joginder's example was a very  extreme case of a small business that  was underfunded  i want to show you um you know a very  simple image of  stacking up the number of corporations  in a developing country and if you do  that the bottom 70  of corporations are micro enterprises  having less than 10 employees and less  than a hundred thousand dollars us  dollars in annual revenue  that's really the focus of microfinance  companies  the top one percent represent the  multinationals and the large enterprises  and uh those are uh you know looked  after by banks  so really you have a you know large  sizable chunk of um percent of the  enterprises in a developing country such  as india which are underfunded  now jorgender's again was an extreme  example given  but i want to kind of talk to you about  what impact these small businesses have  um  not only on an economy such as india but  uh in the world at large  in india small businesses contribute  more than half of the manufacturing  output and half of the exports  coming out of the country that's nearly  20 the gdp of india and yet more than 90  percent of them are self-funded they  lack access to credit  abstracting to the world at large  one-third of the globe's population is  employed by small businesses and they  are underfunded  sitting here in the us you know we think  we're fairly insulated from such  phenomenon  turns out surprisingly that nearly 30  percent of the u.s population has no  savings account  in fact according to forbes  the second biggest concern  on the mind of a small business owner is  access to credit  so i want to do two things in my talk  today  one  i want to share with you my vision for  how financial inclusion can have an  impact on these small businesses  and two  i would ask for your help in helping me  reach out to the lenders of the world  so financial inclusion what about it  wikipedia describes financial inclusion  to be the cost-effective delivery  of financial of affordable financial  services to the poor of the world  i want to give you the example of one  organization  attempting to do this this is my family  business back home in india  magma fincorp  uh we give loans to the underbanked and  the unbanked population in the country  and uh we've been doing this for uh you  know quite long and i think we've  started to get good at it out of every  1000 loans that we give only two loans  go go bad  that's a 0.2 default rate  giving loans to traditionally riskier  customers  having a default rate that's 17 times  better than the public bank in india  this is not by fluke we're actually been  doing this for quite some time and  managing three and a half us billion in  assets  we give large loans  the average ticket size would be close  to around fourteen thousand dollars  these are large loans this allows us to  actually invest the time and effort  required to collect the data because in  india you know you don't have data such  available so readily  and do a lot of capital one style  analytics on this data  and because we're reasonably sure that  our customers will be able to pay us  back we can provide low interest rates  how do we do it  one we get to know our partner really  really well  banks also require paperwork you know  but they're very rigid we actually  provide a choice of 65 different  documents from which the partner can you  know kind of tell us who they are we get  to know how stable they are in that are  they moving their home a lot are they  moving their job a lot we even get to  know their neighborhood and their social  circles that gives us an idea of the  social equity of the person which has an  impact on their repayment rates  second and this is key  is we  evaluate their income in non-traditional  ways so for example for joginder we not  only look at his the size of his farm we  look at how many times he rotates his  crops that gives us an idea of the  output he has from the same amount of  land  we look at um we look at his home uh  joginder has a mud home but he has one  wall in his home that's a brick wall  that tells us something about the person  about the disposable income he had in  the recent past and about his ambitions  in life  next and this is also very key we give  loans for very specific purposes  in fact the loan amount goes directly to  the dealership  the tractor dealership that is providing  the asset hence we are reasonably sure  that the  money is being put to good use  uh one thing that's really critical to  our growth and our success is  innovations on the loan products  uh we try to come up with innovative  products that would meet the needs of  the entrepreneurs in our country  we've come up with a product where even  if the small business cannot provide us  their income statements  we actually provide them 65 percent of  the value of the asset that they're  purchasing for their business based on  just knowing who they are  and ensuring that they're a stable  entrepreneur  we've come up with a new product just  recently where we give a loan against  jewelry in 15 minutes now this is  actually a key uh requirement in the  indian  subcontinent you know you have a lot of  entrepreneurs who you know have a very  urgent need for cash for a recent order  that came in uh you know or they have an  emergency and and this is where this  comes in really handy and we try to keep  innovating on this  great so i've given you a very brief  sneak preview on uh you know how we do  this  and coming to the second part of my talk  i want to ask for your  help i i have a dream i believe that no  dreams should go unfulfilled  because of the lack of funds  and in order to do that in order to  reach out to the vast number of ninjas  in the world and in my country we need  to constantly keep innovating on our  credit algorithms  recently at stanford i got to know for  startup that is um looking at the social  media equity of uh of its uh you know of  customers and they create a credit score  supplement based on just that data  i got to know about a lady you know who  set up a startup which is partnering  with farmers to track their daily income  and their daily expenses  creating a credit score and then putting  them under the formal financial system  so we need to constantly keep innovating  on these algorithms and if you are  passionate about this space if you want  to know more about this space if you  just feel that it'd be nice to do some  research or 390 on this course please  come talk to  me ifc washington uh invests which is a  world bank subsidiary invest in emerging  economies uh invested in us and 